.TH SH-OPTIM 1
.SH NAME
optim \- shell script interface to optim(2), over hoc(2) expressions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load optim

.B id := ${optim new}
.br
.B optim
.I id
.B expr
.I hoc-expression
.br
.B optim
.I id
.B vars
.I name...
.br
.B optim
.I id
.B params
.I name...
.br
.B optim
.I id
.B data
.I x y
.RI [ x y ]...
.br
.B optim
.I id
.B objective
.I coefficient...
.br
.B optim
.I id
.B constraint
.I coefficient... rhs
.br
.B ${optim
.I id
.B value
.I x...
.B }
.br
.B ${optim
.I id
.BR root | bracket | minimise1
.I a b
.B }
.br
.B ${optim
.I id
.B minimise
.I start...
.B }
.br
.B ${optim
.I id
.B fit
.I start...
.B }
.br
.B ${optim
.I id
.B linprog
.B }
.br
.B optim del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Optim
searches, with the function written as a
.IR hoc (2)
expression.
.PP
That is the point of this one.
.IR Optim (2)
takes a function reference and a shell has no functions to give it, so
the objective is compiled from text by
.IR hoc (2)
and evaluated by setting the named variables and calling
.BR eval .
.B optim
.I id
.B root 0 1
on the expression
.B cos(x)-x
is then a complete root-finding session typed at a prompt.
.PP
.B Expr
compiles the expression.
.B Vars
names its variables; one unnamed variable is called
.BR x ,
which is what anyone typing an expression means.
.B Value
evaluates it at a point, which is how to check that what was typed is
what was meant before searching it.
.SS Searching
.B Root
finds where the expression crosses zero between
.I a
and
.IR b ,
which must straddle a root;
.B bracket
widens an interval until it does.
.B Minimise1
returns the
.I x
where it is least between
.I a
and
.IR b ,
and the value there.
.PP
.B Minimise
takes a starting point with one value per variable and returns the
point, then the value, then whether it
.B converged
and how many evaluations it took.
The converged flag is worth reading: Nelder-Mead stopping because it ran
out of steps looks exactly like it finishing.
.SS Fitting and programming
.B Fit
adjusts named
.B params
so the expression matches the
.BR data ,
by least squares.
The expression is then a model in the parameters and the one variable \-
.B a*exp(b*t)
with
.B params a b
and
.BR "vars t" .
It returns the parameters, the sum of squares, the converged flag and
the iteration count.
.PP
.B Linprog
maximises the
.B objective
subject to the
.BR constraint s,
each of which is its coefficients followed by its right-hand side.
Only
.B <=
constraints with a non-negative right-hand side, as in
.IR optim (2).
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
load std
load optim
o := ${optim new}
optim $o expr 'cos(x) - x'
echo ${optim $o value 0.5}	# 0.37758
echo ${optim $o root 0 1}	# 0.73908513321516

optim $o vars x y
optim $o expr '(1-x)*(1-x) + 100*(y-x*x)*(y-x*x)'
echo ${optim $o minimise -1.2 1.0}	# 1 1 4.9e-32 1 340

l := ${optim new}
optim $l objective 3 5
optim $l constraint 1 0 4
optim $l constraint 0 2 12
optim $l constraint 3 2 18
echo ${optim $l linprog}		# 2 6 36 1 3
optim del $o $l
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/optim.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-hoc (1),
.IR sh-stats (1),
.IR optim (2),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR optimtest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An expression that will not compile, a bracket that does not bracket, a
count of starting values that does not match the variables, a
constraint whose width does not match the objective: each fails the
command.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd ,
and no lab in
.B /lib/demo
either \- the other shell interfaces have one, and a search has less to
look at than a waveform or a deflected shape.
.IR Optim (2)
underneath is checked by
.IR optimtest (1),
and what is added here \- the hoc bridge, which is the part worth
testing \- is exercised only by the example above.
.PP
There is one hoc symbol table for the whole shell session, as
.IR sh-hoc (1)
says.
So an objective's variables are global: two handles whose expressions
use
.B x
are using the same
.BR x ,
which does not matter while only one search runs at a time and would
matter very much if one were ever nested inside another.
.PP
The derivative form of
.B newton
in
.IR optim (2)
is not reachable from here, since it needs a second expression.
Use
.BR root ,
which is safeguarded anyway.
